        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Jorge C. Romao work for?\n\nAnswer A: Oak Ridge National Laboratory\n  about: Jorge C. Romao\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 2012\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In 2007, Romao joined Oak Ridge National Laboratory, a premier government research facility in Tennessee, United States.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Instituto Superior Técnico\n  about: Jorge C. Romao\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Romao has had a notable career, with a significant portion of it spent at the Instituto Superior Técnico (IST), a public school of engineering and technology and part of the University of Lisbon.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Jorge C. Romao\n\nJorge C. Romao is a renowned researcher who made significant contributions to the field of materials science during his tenure at Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Early Career\n\nRomao earned his Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering from the University of Tennessee in 2005. His dissertation, titled \"Investigations on the Mechanical Properties of Nanostructured Materials,\" was widely acclaimed and laid the foundation for his future research endeavors. Oak Ridge National Laboratory (2007-2012)\n\nIn 2007, Romao joined Oak Ridge National Laboratory, a premier government research facility in Tennessee, United States. During his five-year stint at the laboratory, he worked closely with the Materials Science and Technology Division, focusing on the development of advanced materials for energy applications. Notable Research Contributions\n\nRomao's research at Oak Ridge National Laboratory centered around the design and synthesis of novel materials with enhanced thermal and electrical conductivity. His work led to several breakthroughs, including the discovery of a new class of thermoelectric materials with improved efficiency. One of his most notable studies, published in the journal Nature Materials in 2010, demonstrated the potential of these materials for waste heat recovery in industrial settings. The study, titled \"High-Performance Thermoelectric Materials for Energy Harvesting,\" was co-authored with his colleagues at Oak Ridge National Laboratory and received widespread attention in the scientific community. Awards and Recognition\n\nRomao's contributions to the field of materials science were recognized with the prestigious Early Career Award in 2011, presented by the Department of Energy's Office of Science. This award honors outstanding researchers who have made significant contributions to their field within the first 10 years of their career. References\n\n* Romao, J. C. (2005). Investigations on the Mechanical Properties of Nanostructured Materials (Ph.D. dissertation). University of Tennessee. * Romao, J. C., et al. (2010). High-Performance Thermoelectric Materials for Energy Harvesting. Nature Materials, 9(10), 841-846. * Department of Energy's Office of Science. (2011). Early Career Award Recipients. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/science.energy.gov\\/early-career\\/>\n\nNote: The above Wikipedia entry is entirely fictional and for demonstration purposes only.\nDocument 2: Here is a Wikipedia-style entry that presents comprehensive evidence for the statement:\n\n**Jorge C. Romao**\n\nJorge C. Romao is a researcher who has made significant contributions to his field. **Career**\n\nRomao has had a notable career, with a significant portion of it spent at the Instituto Superior Técnico (IST), a public school of engineering and technology and part of the University of Lisbon. **Instituto Superior Técnico**\n\nIST is the largest school of engineering in Portugal, boasting a large number of enrolled students, faculty size, scientific production, and patents. As a leading institution in Portugal, IST has a strong reputation for research and academic excellence. **Evidence of Romao's Work at IST**\n\nAccording to multiple credible sources, Jorge C. Romao has worked at Instituto Superior Técnico. These sources include:\n\n* A publication by the European Commission's Community Research and Development Information Service (CORDIS), which lists Romao as a researcher at IST. [1]\n* A research paper published in the Journal of Engineering Research and Applications, which credits Romao as an author and lists his affiliation as IST. [2]\n* A news article by the Portuguese newspaper, Diário de Notícias, which mentions Romao's work at IST. [3]\n\nThese sources provide strong evidence that Jorge C. Romao has worked at Instituto Superior Técnico, a prestigious institution in Portugal. **References**\n\n[1] European Commission's Community Research and Development Information Service (CORDIS). (2010). Researcher Profile: Jorge C. Romao. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/cordis.europa.eu\\/result\\/rcn\\/123456>\n\n[2] Romao, J. C., et al. (2015). \"Research on Advanced Materials.\" Journal of Engineering Research and Applications, 5(3), 123-135. doi: 10.1016\\/j.jera.2015.03.001\n\n[3] Diário de Notícias. (2012). \"IST Researchers Develop New Technology.\" Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.dn.pt\\/sociedade\\/ist-pesquisadores-desenvolvem-nova-tecnologia-2214569.html>\n\nNote: The references provided are fictional and used only for demonstration purposes.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
